WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
In the same week, two stories about
artificial intelligence ran through the

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.200
credit markets, pointing in opposite
directions.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.080
By the end, you should be able to explain
both, and say what evidence would settle

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:11.680
the argument.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.200
The first story is research.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:20.460
On October 1st, 2026, Egan-Jones published
a risk commentary, number 220, called

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.920
"It's Over." Be precise about what that
is.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.160
It is Egan-Jones research and commentary,
not a rating action.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.540
No credit rating changed because of it,
and the piece itself says it was not

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.060
written by credit analysts.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.300
Its argument is that AI has crossed a
threshold, and complete disruption of the

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.140
economy is now close to a certainty.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.460
The first places it says you'll feel that
are professional services fees, venture

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.600
capital, and housing. The second story is
money moving.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.540
Banks working with Broadcom are gathering
about 60 billion dollars of fresh

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.300
financing for AI chips, for Anthropic and
others.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.040
And Anthropic is aiming for an IPO as soon
as mid-November.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.000
So here's the clash in one sentence you
could say back to me.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.840
Lenders are putting tens of billions of
dollars behind building AI, while a

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.160
research firm argues that the same AI will
shrink the revenue of a whole class of

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.840
businesses that lenders also fund.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:23.120
Hold that sentence. Now let's turn a
dramatic title into something a credit

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:24.100
analyst can use.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.610
"It's over" sounds like a slogan.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.650
In credit, we have to make it measurable.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:33.650
A lender cares about two things.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.810
Will the borrower's cash flow pay the debt
back on time?

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.030
And can the borrower borrow again when the
debt comes due?

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.090
So for each part of the Egan-Jones
argument, ask one question: whose cash

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:49.710
flow does this hit? First, how they say
the disruption works: "faster horse versus

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.870
new paradigm." Incumbents use AI to run
existing operations faster.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:55.290
That's the faster horse.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:59.510
Egan-Jones thinks the winners are firms
with very few people, or none, running

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:04.390
agentic work around the clock, citing Grok
Bot, Claude's scheduled cloud tasks, and

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.330
OpenAI following.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.250
Now the first channel: professional
services.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:09.990
This is where they bring numbers.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:16.130
In audit, KPMG secured a 14 percent fee
cut from Grant Thornton UK, from 416,000

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:19.790
dollars to 357,000, citing AI
efficiencies.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.450
In consulting, they point to staff
compression.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:28.690
And they cite an Anthropic claim that
Claude Code can modernize COBOL, the old

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.770
language a lot of large systems still run
on.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:36.530
After that claim, IBM shares fell 13
percent in a day, their worst session

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:41.390
since 2000. Law, investment banking and
engineering, they say, are exposed by the

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:42.030
same logic.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.150
Here's the credit translation, and this
part is mine, not theirs.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.670
Picture a firm that bills by the hour.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:54.190
If AI shrinks the hours, and the client
keeps the savings, the bill shrinks.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.410
A fee cut is a revenue cut.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.870
But the firm's debt doesn't shrink with
its revenue.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.150
The interest payment stays the same size.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:04.790
So the cushion between what the firm earns
and what it owes gets thinner.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.170
And in the KPMG example, notice who kept
the savings.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.370
The client. Second channel: venture
capital.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.610
Egan-Jones argues that if startups scale
with far less human capital, capital

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:21.590
itself matters less, a quiet deflation in
the value of money, in their words.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:27.810
For venture funds, leverage and returns
compress, and they suspect the funds'

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.930
investors, the limited partners, haven't
priced that yet.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.130
I'd label this one speculative.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:36.430
It's an argument about the future, not a
measurement.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.550
Third channel: housing, the most
credit-like of the three, because it runs

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.270
through mortgages.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:47.390
Case-Shiller's national home price index
rose 1.6 percent in the year to June,

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:52.170
against a long-run average increase they
put at about 4.3 percent, while consumer

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.690
prices rose 3.5 percent.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:59.450
My arithmetic: prices rising slower than
inflation means prices falling in real

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:05.810
terms. The median new home cost 4.7 times
median household income in 2025, versus

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.970
3.6 times in 1984.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.990
And about 60 percent of owner-occupied
homes carry a mortgage.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:15.650
Their worry is the two-income household
with a thin cushion.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.110
Disrupt one income, and they see forced
sales over six to twelve months:

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:24.850
short-term downward pressure on prices,
while long-run scarcity of urban land

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:25.670
still holds.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:28.470
That's forward-looking, and it's theirs.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.410
So what would "over" do to the three
things a credit student watches:

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.030
spreads, maturities, and refinancing?

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:40.070
A spread is the extra yield a borrower
pays over a Treasury bond, to compensate

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.090
the lender for the risk of not being paid.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:47.510
If lenders come to believe a borrower's
revenue is shrinking for good, not for a

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:49.450
quarter, they demand more compensation.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:51.350
Spreads widen.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:53.770
Maturity is when the loan comes due.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:58.310
When lenders doubt a business model will
exist in its current form a few years out,

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.110
they lend for shorter periods, or not at
all.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.650
Shorter is safer for the lender and harder
on the borrower.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.180
Refinancing is where those meet.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.080
Most companies don't repay debt out of
savings.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.700
They roll it, issuing new debt to repay
the old.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.700
If spreads are wider and terms are shorter
right when the old debt comes due,

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.480
refinancing gets more expensive, or
doesn't happen.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.820
That's when "over" would actually show up
in credit.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.500
Not the day a research note is published,
but the day a disrupted borrower needs to

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.280
roll its debt. One caution.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:36.560
Egan-Jones attached no spreads or ratings
to any of this, and neither will I.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.640
That was a mechanism, not a forecast.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.580
So that's the disruption side.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.160
Now the lenders funding the buildout, and
for this part, picture a diagram.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.560
Let's walk the picture left to right.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.300
On the left, the lenders.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.120
Per Bloomberg's Markets Daily and Morning
Briefing, banks working with Broadcom are

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:58.360
gathering about 60 billion dollars of
fresh AI chip financing.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.520
In the middle, the borrowers: Anthropic
and others.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:06.300
The reporting gives no collateral terms,
rate, or maturity, so neither will I.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.520
In credit, an unknown term is a real
unknown.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.820
What we know is what the money buys:

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:15.780
chips. The second borrower box is Amazon.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:20.500
Morning Briefing, citing the FT, says
Amazon is pursuing an asset-light chip

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.720
financing that keeps about 8 billion
dollars off its books, meaning the debt

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.100
doesn't sit on Amazon's own balance sheet.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:29.960
Above the middle sits the equity layer.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:35.240
Anthropic is aiming for an IPO as soon as
mid-November, with valuation chatter up to

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.830
about 2 trillion dollars.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:42.220
Why does a lender care? Equity is paid
after debt, so it cushions the lenders

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.180
only if it is actually raised and sits
junior to them.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.180
An aim and a valuation rumor are not that
cushion yet.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:55.400
Now the bar underneath: the same credit
market every other borrower is priced in.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.100
Here's what it said that week.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.440
First reading: the US CCC-and-lower
spread, the riskiest slice of the junk

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.500
bond market, closed September 30th at
1,007 basis points.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:11.220
That's up from about 860 at the start of
September, and the first print above a

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.820
thousand since March 2023.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:18.080
Odd Lots described the shape: high yield
blowing out against investment grade, and

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.200
within high yield, CCCs blowing out while
single-Bs and double-Bs lag.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:26.680
That's a quality split. The market
punishes the weakest borrowers and leaves

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:28.260
the middle relatively calm.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.360
Second reading: Paramount.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.200
Not an AI deal, which is why it's useful.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.680
Paramount's jumbo M&A bonds slumped on
their debut.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:42.340
Matt Levine sizes them at 41 billion
dollars, Markets Daily at 52 billion, and

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:44.120
I'll give you both rather than pick one.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:49.860
The lesson: the market's capacity for very
large deals is real, but it has a price,

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:51.640
and that price is rising.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.420
Third reading: the base rate under every
spread.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:01.620
The ten-year Treasury was around 5.23 to
5.24 percent in October 1st and 2nd

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:03.620
snapshots, near a 24-year high.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.220
So the strongest AI borrowers are raising
money in a market that still lends to

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:09.620
quality, at high base rates.

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:14.460
The open question is how far down the
quality ladder this financing reaches.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.420
Last, the red arrow on the right, which is
the point of the whole diagram.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.400
The chip financing is a bet that AI demand
is real and large.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:28.020
The disrupted borrowers are businesses
whose billable hours, Egan-Jones argues,

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.600
are exactly what that AI will compress.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:35.840
If the buildout succeeds, the builders'
debt looks great and some disrupted

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.240
borrowers' debt looks worse.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.560
If it disappoints, that runs the other
way.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:44.780
Either way, both loans hinge on one
variable: whether AI works as advertised.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:48.040
A lender holding both is less diversified
than it looks.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:50.060
That leaves the last step.

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.260
If you had to referee this argument, what
would you watch?

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:56.950
Start with Egan-Jones.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:01.330
For "over" to show up in credit, the data
would have to change in four ways.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:04.670
First, the fee cut would have to stop
being one example.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.370
KPMG and Grant Thornton UK is a single
contract.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:10.390
You'd want repricing across audit,

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:14.270
consulting, law and banking, with clients
keeping the savings.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.670
Second, the spread widening would have to
land on the right borrowers.

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:23.810
CCCs are above a thousand, but I don't
have a sector breakdown of that index, so

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:27.430
I can't tell you how much of the stress
sits in labor-heavy service businesses.

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:29.950
That breakdown is the real test.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:33.990
Third, housing would have to move as they
describe:

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.830
prices keep trailing inflation, and forced
sales appear.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:43.070
Beside that, Evening Briefing had the
mortgage rate at 7.28 percent, the highest

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:48.890
since November 2023. And since the housing
channel runs through incomes, watch jobs.

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:55.090
September payrolls added only 29,000, and
unemployment rose to 4.2 percent.

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:57.830
But nothing in the data attributes that to
AI.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:09:59.950
Fourth, private marks.

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:05.530
Apollo is moving to daily marks across its
850 billion dollar credit book by the end

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:10.270
of October, so private credit prices will
start showing what the public CCC tape

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:15.470
already shows. And watch default rates,
counted carefully: KBRA's count-based

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:20.370
measure is around 3.5 percent, Fitch's
broader measure around 6 or more.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:21.830
Track each one's direction.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.990
Don't merge them. Now the financing side.

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:27.750
For the buildout lenders to be right,
you'd see the opposite.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:31.610
The 60 billion gets placed without
Paramount's indigestion.

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:36.430
The Anthropic IPO happens, putting that
equity cushion under the lenders.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:38.110
The quality split holds.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:43.650
And the quiet one: service businesses keep
refinancing at stable spreads and

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:46.830
maturities, which would say their revenue
isn't shrinking the way "It's Over"

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:47.330
argues.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.950
The uncomfortable last point: both sides
can be right at once.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:56.430
The builders can be repaid in full
precisely because AI does what Egan-Jones

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:56.890
fears.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.150
Credit is underwriting both sides of the
same trade.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:05.890
To recap. In credit, "over" means wider
spreads, shorter maturities, and harder

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:07.870
refinancing for disrupted borrowers.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:12.350
The buildout means about 60 billion
dollars of chip financing in a market

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:13.950
already splitting by quality.

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:16.090
And the referee is the data:

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.850
where the widening lands, and who can
still roll their debt.

